well good afternoon it's a tremendously  powerful report that Alex has done it  speaks to the daily reality that we in  the International Rescue Committee see  both in countries in the Middle East and  in Africa where failed States are unable  to contain conflict and now on the  shores of Europe where people are  exploiting the desperation I think that  was the word she used and it's  absolutely right the desperation of  people and the fact that there aren't  properly organized and ordered routes  for people either to be returned to  their homes if they are not suffering  from the threat of persecution or for  them to be resettled in cases where they  do have that well-founded fear of  persecution that is the definition of a  refugee all of the evidence that we have  from people who talk to us is the fact  that these wars in Somalia going on for  30 years in Afghanistan 25 years now the  Syrian conflict on top are overwhelming  the systems that exist and how do you  make that decision whether people should  be helped to resettle or in fact they  should be sent home the United Nations  has a very well-established definition  of what it is to be a refugee was APIs  tablished after the Second World War the  definition is that someone who has a  well-founded fear of persecution is a  refugee as opposed to an economic  migrants and in your introductory  comments about 20 million of them  globally are refugees there's one  refugee every five or six seconds at the  moment because of the number of civil  wars that are going around the world but  twice that number are internally  displaced people in countries in the  Middle East but also across Africa where  we work we see lots of people who are  displaced within their own homes and the  obvious response is that at a time when  humanitarian aid is under pressure we  need to be doing more if you like  upstream in countries in the Middle East  in Africa as well because if you don't  make the humanitarian efforts upstream  to provide shelter to provide health  care to provide education if you don't  do the work there then the sense of  desperation will grow as I saw for  myself in nazare which is a country in  the middle of Africa it's a trade route  I'm sorry to use that word for  or people who are being smuggled across  Africa and if there isn't proper  provision in countries like that to halt  the flow of people then they will end up  on the shores of Europe or or elsewhere  you're spoken of doing more work  upstream as you describe it you're the  CEO of International Rescue Committee  and what is your organization doing to  prevent this humanitarian crisis when we  have about 15,000 staff in 30 countries  around the world we're working to  provide health care to provide education  for kids to ensure there's proper water  and sanitation and it's a telltale sign  of how desperate things are that we've  had to deploy some of our staff to the  greek island of lesbos where a thousand  people are arriving a day and wearing  one of the transit centers there were  five toilets for 3,000 people but the  vast bulk of our work is in countries  like Lebanon Jordan Turkey Iraq in the  Middle East in Congo in nazare as I say  where I was recently and in Somalia  itself so we're working to try to make  sure the conditions that people are  forced to live in are in fact livable  and that there is a basic modicum of  dignity for them there it has to be said  that at a time when there are 60 million  people fleeing conflict at the moment it  too often feels like a losing battle and  that's why the calls which to be fair  have been led by british governments of  both parties for international aid to be  stepped up are well-founded but it does  sound quite reactive i mean you can't  prevent the wars you can't prevent the  conflict which really is the real cause  isn't it you're absolutely right to say  that but i would say two things partly  on the basis of my experiences of  foreign minister but also now is in my  experience as the chief executive of a  humanitarian organization we need far  more effort on the diplomatic front to  tend to those wars that do seem endless  I mean if you take the stereo example  that's the largest humanitarian conflict  in the world moment it's produced more  refugees 4 million refugees from that  conflict into neighboring countries but  the whole diplomatic effort is one UN  official trying to broker temporary  ceasefire in the moment  absence of a drive for a political  solution in Syria the sense of  desperation in other conflicts where UN  peacekeeping forces are underfunded and  undermanned that's what creates the  conditions where these conflicts spill  over not just into neighboring states  but onto the shores of Europe so the  first thing I would say is that it needs  to be a far greater level of diplomatic  attention political attention to the  conflicts themselves we can't just throw  up our hands and say that they're  insoluble the other thing to do of  course and to pick up your word  humanitarian organizations like ours  need to plan better we need to be sure  that we're not simply in the reactive  mode that you described and that's why  organizations like IRC we're doubling  our emergency capacity with pre  positioning people and goods to make  sure that we can respond within the 72  hours that we're pledged to do so and  and in your view does that international  attention which perhaps might be lacking  at the moment does that include military  intervention we're a humanitarian  organization and we look to political  settlements rather than military ones  the vital thing physical the political  settlement might be that perhaps  military intervention is what is needed  as a last resort as it might be well  obviously there's a in the cook in the  conflicts where we're providing  humanitarian help whether in the Middle  East or elsewhere there's a range of  military actors and interventions some  of them from governments some of them  from non-state jihadist players and we  have to deal with the consequences as a  humanitarian organization obviously the  key thing in the Syria crisis though is  that for four years there has been no  agreed political vision for the future  of Syria even the Geneva talks that were  brokered by Kofi Annan the former sector  general the UN broke up within days of  their conclusion with contending views  about what had been agreed and it's the  absence of that political settlement  that I think has empowered a whole range  of radical and other groups and led to  the fracturing of Syrian society in the  most appalling way in your view then has  the international community failed these  vulnerable people is there something  that they're not doing that they should  be as of today  there's definitely a collective failure  no one could look at the scenes that you  are reporting and see anything other  than first a failure to tackle the  problems at their root cause secondly a  failure to establish effective  humanitarian protection in the countries  that are in conflict or in the  neighboring states which is the best  place to catch people to meet their  needs and thirdly there's obviously been  a collective failure in Europe as well  when it comes to organizing proper  provision for people who are fleeing I  should point out to a European audience  that in the United States there was a  similar sort of case last summer with  tens of thousands of children fleeing  from three central american states  including Guatemala and El Salvador and  the kind of scenes that you're seeing in  Europe were seen on land at US border  crossings last year so this is a global  problem it needs a far more systemic  global response and it needs to buttress  the efforts of organizations like the  International Rescue Committee  organizations like ours which are on the  ground it needs to buttress those  efforts with far more effective  political intervention otherwise we're  forever running to stand still now  people at home will live watched Alex  Crawford's report and have seen that the  detention centers while actually holding  pens we're probably glorifying them  calling them detention centers and  wondered why it excuse me there isn't  anything that we can do to help these  people when we know the condition  they're in and where they are we've  found it very very difficult to get into  Libya for the kind of security reasons  that Alex Crawford referred to however I  can speak to the situation in Greece  where we've had to deploy some of our  teams and we precisely are attending in  what you rightly call their you call  them holding pens detention centres I  mean I think we know that they fall far  short of anything that could be  considered a basic level of organization  and living we're literally building  sanitation facilities water provision  because without that then there's even  more desperation and even more incentive  to be carried across the rest of Europe  so there are there is work going on my  organization others are due  important aspects of that but I think  the critical message that's got to go  out is that there needs to be upstream  help as well as intervention on the  shores of Europe we're actually on the  borders of Italy and Greece where you  see some of these appalling scenes and  there are recent reports some of the  attempts to save people in the waters of  off Europe becoming a bit less  successful there's been progress over  the last few months for those are there  been some reports that they're actually  faltering I think it's very important  that a message of very real clarity goes  out on the back of the report that  you've done that actually there are more  people are going to come out of  desperation unless their basic  conditions are addressed

